{"type": "survey_submit", "survey_type": "post", "puzzle_idx": null, "answers": {"puzzle_1_rate_again": 3, "puzzle_1_guesses": "never", "puzzle_2_rate_again": 4, "puzzle_2_guesses": "never", "puzzle_3_rate_again": 2, "puzzle_3_guesses": "never", "puzzle_1_rating_reason": "The beginning steps had some guaranteed patterns where I could fill out some entire line/rows. After that, there were some steps where I had to consider multiple different possible patterns to find guaranteed blocks among them. So I considered the difficulty moderate.", "puzzle_2_rating_reason": "This puzzle started with only one (if I didn't miss any) line with guaranteed pattern. For the rest line/rows, I had to consider some non-trivial guaranteed blocks. For example, I found out two guaranteed blocks from a line of \"2 2 1 1\", but I had to go over more possible patterns than a line with large numbers such as \"4 1\".", "puzzle_3_rating_reason": "This puzzle provided two guaranteed line&row on the edge, which thus gave more information about the blocks connected to them. But in general it is not considered very easy.", "strategy": "I would first look for lines/rows with guaranteed solution and fill them up. Then, based on the filled blocks, I try to fill the blocks next to them. For example, if a block on the edge is filled with guaranteed solution, I expand it to the centre based on what its line says about the first/last number, and cross out the one next to it. Also, some pattern would have non-trivial guaranteed blocks, I actively check all possible solutions and find the common filled/crossed-out blocks. (e.g. \"6 1\" would have some guaranteed blocks filled out near the middle)", "comments": "When there's more steps where you have to start consider different possibilities, it feels harder. When there's more straight-forward guaranteed blocks, it feels easier (e.g. cross out a block around guaranteed filled blocks, fill blocks near a filled edge block according to the number)."}, "t": "202